#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188024 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "d97c3cf2-b627-5274-853a-be3d7e5cda40" :question/math-academy-id "e-10246" :question/problem "![](images/02/025ea896b8ef2253530a6392fc5b5c89c70df5083a673de14a1c6e13108068bd.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,2].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of the function $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,2]$ given that $f(2) = 4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-6,2),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nThe function is made up of $1$ trapezoid and $1$ triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1bcd6d6800caf6abdca868bba4abff3935f26e984807f13c25d4368dc858f2c1.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2) & =4 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-8 \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(2)+{\\int }_{2}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(2)-{\\int }_{-6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4-(-6+8) \\\\  & =4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{4,-4,2\\} = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/id #uuid "b28b6939-847d-51ab-a97d-4367323d0d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-10247" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-6,6].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9, 3$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-6,6]$ given that $f(6) = 2.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab98f2f27ff11ddb40138bf09900a865ca006bc8f26c95dfe03ea17fd4e49387.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-6,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-6,6),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-6,0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =2 \\\\ f(3) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-5) \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(0) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(3-5) \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-6) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-6}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-(-9+3-5) \\\\  & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{2, 7, 4, 13\\} = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = 2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = 6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "71cc1f65-437f-5928-82c2-be3dc4b0985f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10248" :question/problem "![](images/69/69d2d66022521424f9c6cda6ad901eb9ed1dbdb01044f5fa2d2954049770a3d4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,4].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $9,$ $7$ and $8,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,4]$ given that $f(1) = 10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-4,4),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,1,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =10 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-2}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-7) \\\\  & =10+7 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(-4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{-4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =f(1)-{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10-(-9-7) \\\\  & =10-(-16) \\\\  & =10+16 \\\\  & =26 \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10+8 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{10,17,26,18\\} = 26.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{max} = 26,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is  $x_\\text{max} = -4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/id #uuid "708db737-4215-5df0-8940-c9d17dbd2b5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10249" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,5].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $14$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,5]$ given that $f(3) = -2.$\n\n![](images/de/deb5d80fe70d31fe768a180f3afd8a6054eda8e9fc3d1d0cd52c696c9de47660.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points.\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x \\in (-5,5),$ and $f'(x) = 0$ at $x=-3$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-3,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-2) \\\\  & =-4 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-14 \\\\  & =-16 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-2+14) \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{min}} = \\min\\{-2, -4, -16, -14\\} = -16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is $f_\\text{min} = -16,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{min} = -3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b298866-d4d3-50ef-99c2-892e1ccbad3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524459} {:answer/id #uuid "43752373-0228-5044-b688-cb27a6eeb3f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524504} {:answer/id #uuid "68878cf0-9180-5c61-9b7c-7781cf21cc65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524553} {:answer/id #uuid "8970f9a2-de34-5b1a-af61-3790f8b31e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524599} {:answer/id #uuid "e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5b58c4c-fba7-5228-b103-b2cb83d6e1d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524784} :answer-field/id #uuid "891a58fe-e347-55e1-9227-fafd48e4c74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9080bcff-95c0-5480-aa15-7e41729ae6e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-104260" :question/problem "![](images/39/39ee1ed9a272b610d17b52f5225a1fcbcfa9966e0eb083dd8c9d53b1cd29b8b9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $11,$ $2$ and $2,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-5,4]$ if $f(0)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-5,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,0,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =-2 \\\\ f(-5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-5}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-11) \\\\  & =9 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(2-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,9,0,-2\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the values of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ are ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0,4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188527 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188320 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f2efc16-254b-5723-937e-60ad37132c98" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9eca73a-1249-5ae1-b362-ffc094617276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185443} {:answer/id #uuid "c7b4cbdf-1577-5a6a-99f5-35e017e43b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185444} {:answer/id #uuid "1345e68d-d5bf-5260-bf4d-cd225fed96a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185445} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb6dbb3-fbbe-59d2-9fe0-e3e1bfcc09bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185446} {:answer/id #uuid "5e94f044-8c76-5981-9173-610f747c5013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9eca73a-1249-5ae1-b362-ffc094617276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185443} :answer-field/id #uuid "d153408e-bf16-54d4-a353-ba2449e79d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ab229b6-ba1f-5ab4-bb83-4e18f639baa1" :question/math-academy-id "q-104271" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fedd81f87921d865c375ecdbf9b83439092d138686e07753bb20ae522b6e49c9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(0)=56.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $2$ rectangles and $2$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/96/962bea79c8308d1d63937aa15f19492f2dd0c31f278e2957a71255dbbd92a8ca.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =56 \\\\ f(-3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{-3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(0)-{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56-(-2-2) \\\\  & =60 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =56+2+2 \\\\  & =60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{56,60,60\\}=56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=56,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0077e303-78c8-5a4d-9f4e-3d35cf42cb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524378} {:answer/id #uuid "98599ccb-165d-5a9f-89f7-6a0fa52e415c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524626} {:answer/id #uuid "9b90e5cf-272b-5399-83d6-f0c4cec499fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524631} {:answer/id #uuid "9d3dcf13-c55f-5e57-ab59-e148431cea27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524632} {:answer/id #uuid "cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb14986c-8086-5ecf-ba35-3b4e666cd1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524716} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5d30eaa-4a91-54ae-a4e2-bfc408fea6d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba02335d-7784-52da-a978-380fc6e80144" :question/math-academy-id "q-144840" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c79d3a4de46d41da5edec58514bd7138ff3369032c76c758d57d3816f99e3ef3.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,6]$ given that $f(6)=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =-2 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2-(-10) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,8\\}=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fdfdccb-f867-5185-8ab5-657123634d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524517} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7c51fa-cfb6-5f55-b174-77dad9d56fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524658} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ec6c24-2292-511b-9be5-127f37247732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524697} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524727} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ea1700-7cfc-5ba3-a0a5-c241917d08b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524788}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfc7aaea-4301-584f-bdc3-af424dfb7c9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524727} :answer-field/id #uuid "2595da81-f0d2-506b-9398-dff018e8c2ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c49e6b-d082-5bf8-805d-249ef4b78827" :question/math-academy-id "q-144845" :question/problem "![](images/69/69ae26109021b156ae22879f30a64c393fa9a870d207be72c75533c8b6918bb9.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2$ and $5,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,6]$ given that $f(6)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(6) & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{2}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(1) & =f(6)+{\\int }_{6}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(6)-{\\int }_{1}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-(5-2) \\\\  & =3-3 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,-2,0\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188318 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "373ecc37-7913-5b59-adea-7ad3d5d78f28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Minimum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b8fe3d-8331-5609-a33b-334910eff9b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524392} {:answer/id #uuid "261a9480-07c6-5439-8161-94b991801e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524454} {:answer/id #uuid "2be118f0-398f-5be1-86b7-522a08c5d5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524461} {:answer/id #uuid "6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524556} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e80193-25cd-5cb0-a082-8787876542f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6be9a124-f36b-5039-beb4-9169cea0786a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524556} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c5f3fbc-3413-5fe3-a14b-7220c2d641bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59718fdb-33b2-584f-912f-49ef25f56ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-144867" :question/problem "![](images/89/89445981059ca7902f22c916f7241573dcfa8b70798b067896b9a30d0913d251.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $3$ and $9,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,5]$ given that $f(5)=10.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5) & =10 \\\\ f(1) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-9 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(5)+{\\int }_{5}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =f(5)-{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10-(-3+9) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{10,1,4\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188317 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81dea8fe-5a5b-5a29-9aaa-2c0d4f1764e9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at the Right Endpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83a91dc7-6957-5530-aee4-3045d16a9de2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524586} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d31afb4-f8c1-5171-921e-f2991ad5424b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3b6054b-f3bd-5aa9-aa30-6fbb1ec1ef44" :question/math-academy-id "q-344095" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cad4c483d2678c63382c13d43e3377c0a471ff510bd6b86ea37dad3adb440d9a.png)\n\nThe function $p(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${p}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $p(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $9.$\n\nGiven that $p(3)=-4,$ what is the maximum value ${p}_{\\text{max}}$ of $p(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]?$\n\n${p}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the maximum value of $p(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${p}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${p}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ inside the interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $p$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(3) & =-4 \\\\ p(-5) & =p(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-5}{p}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =p(3)-{\\int }_{-5}^{3}{p}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4-9 \\\\  & =-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $p$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $p$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{p}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-4,-13\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $p$ is ${p}_{\\text{max}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $p(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dcfb3be-68ac-5ef8-ac92-f7ed17e3cd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524634} :answer-field/id #uuid "36e143a5-6376-5cd6-8c7d-32d7f7188f06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5944661-3e94-5778-ad45-da56e2af2415" :question/math-academy-id "q-344398" :question/problem "![](images/66/6613de40d3150985d471d97fd3b5369cd67df2ae248b22ce99af011f3484a20c.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-5,3].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5,$ $4,$ and $7$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-5,3]$ given that $g(-2)=3.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-5,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-5,3),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2,0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-5,-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-2) & =3 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3+4 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ g(3) & =g(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+(-7) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ g(-5) & =g(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =g(-2)-{\\int }_{-5}^{-2}{g}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =3-5 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{3,7,0,-2\\}=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186525002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188319 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b22c6bd-c098-53af-bac1-500de0b75ed2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph and a Value at an Interior Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5f0e03b-b91a-5f40-83a8-85dcfb2ab7ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524700} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec89a11-249d-5e23-acad-e5fe2601910b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186524897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "839781ca-f17e-59bc-bcb0-dc6733a65b9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344414" :question/problem "![](images/24/24ee434f11bca408587e1baa6b9189e430fa745cef0668bc35cd871761f410ed.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $2,$ $6,$ and $4$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $h(3)=2.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1,3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,-1,3,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =2 \\\\ h(5) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{5}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(3)-{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =2-(-6) \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(-3) & =h(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{-3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =h(-1)-{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =8-(-2) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,8,10\\}=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=10,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$"}]}
